          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Before clipping the gallbladder's blood supply during cholecystectomy, the surge</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Before clipping the gallbladder's blood supply during cholecystectomy, the surgeon must identify the cystic artery. Within which anatomical space does this artery lie?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hepatorenal pouch of Morrison</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Morrison's pouch is the deepest recess between the liver and the right kidney — it collects fluid in supine patients but does not contain the cystic artery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Epiploic foramen of Winslow</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The epiploic foramen connects the greater and lesser sacs. The cystic artery lies within Calot's triangle (hepatocystic triangle), not the epiploic foramen.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Calot's triangle</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The cystic artery, which is the sole blood supply to the gallbladder, lies within Calot's triangle (also called the hepatocystic triangle). Precise identification here prevents catastrophic ligation of the right hepatic artery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lesser sac (omental bursa)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The lesser sac lies behind the stomach and communicates with the greater sac via the epiploic foramen. The cystic artery is located within Calot's triangle, not the lesser sac.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The cystic artery lies within Calot's triangle (hepatocystic triangle). The surgeon must identify it here before ligation to avoid accidentally clipping the right hepatic artery."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Calot's triangle — bounded by the cystic duct, common hepatic duct, and the inferior surface of the liver — is the critical surgical landmark for identifying the cystic artery during cholecystectomy."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
