          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with massive upper gastrointestinal haemorrhage from a peptic</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with massive upper gastrointestinal haemorrhage from a peptic ulcer on the posterior wall of the first part of the duodenum. Which artery has the ulcer most likely eroded?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Left gastric artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach. It is not directly related to the posterior wall of the first part of the duodenum. Posterior duodenal ulcers erode the gastroduodenal artery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gastroduodenal artery</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The gastroduodenal artery lies immediately behind the first part of the duodenum. Posterior wall ulcers erode into this vessel, causing severe haemorrhage — in contrast to anterior wall ulcers, which tend to perforate into the peritoneal cavity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Superior mesenteric artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The superior mesenteric artery is related to the third part of the duodenum, where it can cause SMA syndrome. Posterior ulcers of the duodenal cap erode the gastroduodenal artery, not the SMA.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Splenic artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The splenic artery runs along the superior border of the pancreas towards the spleen. It has no direct contact with the first part of the duodenum. The vessel at risk from posterior duodenal ulcers is the gastroduodenal artery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The gastroduodenal artery lies directly behind the first part of the duodenum. Posterior wall ulcers erode into it, causing life-threatening haemorrhage."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The gastroduodenal artery sits directly behind the duodenal cap. This anatomical relationship explains why posterior ulcers bleed (arterial erosion) while anterior ulcers perforate (peritoneal breach)."
                }
              ],
