          "label": "<p>A patient with a subphrenic abscess reports pain at the shoulder tip despite hav</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with a subphrenic abscess reports pain at the shoulder tip despite having no shoulder pathology. Which nerve mediates this referred pain, known as Kehr's sign?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Vagus nerve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The vagus nerve passes through the oesophageal hiatus at T10 and supplies foregut viscera. It does not carry somatic sensation from the diaphragm to the shoulder. Kehr's sign is mediated by the phrenic nerve (C3–C5).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Greater splanchnic nerve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The greater splanchnic nerve carries sympathetic fibres to the coeliac plexus and does not supply the diaphragm's central portion. Kehr's sign arises because the phrenic nerve (C3–C5) shares its root value with the shoulder's C4 dermatome.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Phrenic nerve</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The phrenic nerve (C3, C4, C5) supplies the central diaphragm. Subdiaphragmatic irritation stimulates this nerve, and the brain refers the pain to the C4 dermatome — the shoulder tip. This is seen in splenic rupture and subphrenic abscess.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lower intercostal nerves</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The lower intercostal nerves supply the peripheral diaphragm, but Kehr's sign — referred shoulder tip pain — is mediated by the phrenic nerve (C3–C5), which shares the C4 root value with the shoulder tip dermatome.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Kehr's sign is mediated by the phrenic nerve (C3, C4, C5). Irritation beneath the diaphragm stimulates this nerve, and the brain interprets the signal as arising from the C4 dermatome at the shoulder tip."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Kehr's sign results from phrenic nerve irritation: its C3–C5 root value overlaps with the shoulder tip's C4 dermatome, so subdiaphragmatic blood or pus produces referred shoulder pain."
                }
              ],
